{% doc %}
  Product card for every product grid and strip on the site — collection,
  search, "You may like". The final layout (Eugenia's PDFs, 2026-09-24) is
  the photograph with the product's name laid over its bottom-left corner and
  nothing under it: no price, no category, no colour swatches. The price
  lives on the product page only, in the add-to-cart button. The same name
  format is used by the curated cards on the homepage and the category pages
  (featured-card), so a product looks the same wherever it appears.

  On a real pointer the second photograph fades in on hover (see base.css).

  @param {product} product - the product to render
  @param {boolean} [featured] - oversized lead tile (spans 2×2 grid cells on
    desktop); also loads its image eagerly
  @example
  {% render 'product-card', product: product %}
  {% render 'product-card', product: product, featured: true %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  comment
    Card widths written out rather than approximated with one vw figure, and
    solved for a gap of ZERO: that is the widest a card can ever be, and a
    `sizes` that comes out under the real slot is exactly what serves a
    too-small file. The grid sits inside the page margins (40px each side —
    the left gutter the stacked nav needed is gone at every width), so:
      >= 990px : four columns of (100vw - 80px)
      750-989  : two columns
      <  750px : the widest a phone card gets, one column
    The featured tile spans two columns.
  endcomment
  assign card_sizes = '(min-width: 990px) calc(25vw - 20px), (min-width: 750px) calc(50vw - 40px), calc(100vw - 80px)'
  assign card_sizes_featured = '(min-width: 990px) calc(50vw - 40px), calc(100vw - 80px)'
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  The hover photograph (Cult Gaia): the product's SECOND image, stacked on
  the first and faded in when a mouse is over the card. It is a second <img>
  rather than a swapped src so the browser has it decoded before the pointer
  arrives — a swap shows a blank frame on the first hover. Only rendered when
  the product actually has a second image, and only shown where a real
  pointer can hover (see base.css); touch never sees it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign hover_media = product.images[1] -%}

<article class="product-card{% if featured %} product-card--featured{% endif %}" data-product-card>
  <a href="{{ product.url }}" class="product-card__link" data-card-link>
    <div class="product-card__media">
      {%- if product.featured_media -%}
        {%- if featured -%}
          {{-
            product.featured_media
            | image_url: width: 2400
            | image_tag:
              widths: '550, 750, 1100, 1500, 2000, 2400',
              sizes: card_sizes_featured,
              loading: 'eager',
              fetchpriority: 'high',
              class: 'product-card__image'
          -}}
        {%- else -%}
          {{-
            product.featured_media
            | image_url: width: 1600
            | image_tag:
              widths: '360, 540, 720, 900, 1200, 1600',
              sizes: card_sizes,
              loading: 'lazy',
              class: 'product-card__image'
          -}}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- else -%}
        <div class="product-card__media--placeholder" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if hover_media and product.featured_media -%}
        {{-
          hover_media
          | image_url: width: 1600
          | image_tag:
            widths: '360, 540, 720, 900, 1200, 1600',
            sizes: card_sizes,
            loading: 'lazy',
            alt: '',
            class: 'product-card__image product-card__image--hover',
            aria-hidden: 'true',
            data-card-hover-image: ''
        -}}
      {%- endif -%}

      <h3 class="product-card__title">{{ product.title }}</h3>
    </div>

  </a>
</article>
